{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '210,239p' PLAN.md && sed -n '150,193p' Auto-Lap-Happy-Auto-Racing-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-041321/vendor/engine/modules/drive.js && head -35 Auto-Lap-Happy-Auto-Racing-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-041321/vendor/engine/my-3d2dge.js\"","aggregated_output":"| `src/dev/` | Inspectors, live tuning, overlays, reproducible test bridge |\n| `tools/build.mjs` | Validates data and emits the prescribed single-file deliverable |\n| `tools/test.mjs` | One quiet entry point for applicable automated checks |\n| `tests/` | Rule, simulation, input, browser, audio, network, packaging, and complete-playthrough checks |\n| `docs/` | Research, architecture/authoring notes, issue ledger, validation evidence, feedback notes |\n\nExpose a small, read-only runtime snapshot and deliberate test actions: get current state, advance controlled simulation where supported, set a test input intent, and read errors/events. Keep it separate from competitive authority and unavailable as an online cheat. Avoid tests coupled to transient draw calls or DOM implementation details.\n\n### Known engine fixes to address early\n\n1. **Countdown and stopping:** the existing race countdown supplies brake from rest, while the car model can reverse under braking. Anchor/freeze grid bodies before GO and verify no reverse creep. Recovery staging needs the same protection. During ordinary driving, holding brake at rest must hold zero speed; releasing must resume auto acceleration.\n2. **Analog input:** the input-instance path can turn trigger values into booleans. Normalize with `input.value(action)` and pass explicit `{steer,gas,brake,drift}` intents so intermediate pressure survives. Drift remains zero unless an authored handling feature needs it; no kart boost.\n3. **Progress:** replace or supplement global `path.nearest` and distance-bin checkpoints with the directed-gate/road-edge system above. Keep one authoritative progression owner.\n4. **HUD:** adapt the default kart HUD; remove item slots and replace tiny information with the sparse TV-readable race HUD.\n5. **Contact:** verify circular car collisions for coincident bodies, stationary contact, and fast scenery impacts. Improve the model only where observed failures require it.\n6. **Pause and input:** extend native menu/focus/bindings rather than stacking another incompatible pause/input loop. Use live overlays online and disable automatic focus/visibility/controller-loss pausing for network authority.\n\nUpstream generic instructions that conflict with this build’s required Hub integration, permitted dependencies, or installed-browser rules do not override the build brief. Do not download browsers or install another game engine.\n\n## 10. Dev mode, saves, and attract mode\n\nDev mode is reachable from pause with normal controls. Include course/car selection, restart at gate, free camera, invulnerability for solo testing, AI driving, seed display, gate/collision/route overlays, state inspection, and a way to finish or reset the current tour step. Make enabled cheats obvious and mark affected records unranked.\n\nExpose important balance values as real live sliders: speed scale, acceleration, braking, steering, road/grass/gravel grip, crash threshold, AI pace, lead distance, and duel score target. Show value and default, apply changes at safe boundaries when required, and include a reliable reset-to-defaults action. Shared matched profiles remain matched. Do not allow remote competitive clients to change rules; a host may select a new agreed preset between standalone matches, not mid-round.\n\nVersion and validate settings, discoveries, personal bests, and ghost data. Recover from absent, corrupt, or incompatible storage. Storage failure under `file://` must not block playing. Separate ordinary player records, test/dev records, and transient Hub session storage.\n\nAfter **10 seconds without meaningful input on standalone title/menu screens**, enter a looping AI-played showcase. Show road driving, all five course identities, a time-trial finish, an exploration connection, and duel scoring/restarts across the loop. A subtle DEMO indicator and one clear exit prompt are enough. Any meaningful input exits immediately and is consumed, restoring the prior menu/focus; it must not also launch a race or change a selection. Preserve settings, saves, and any pending connection setup, and make the attract run unable to write records or unlocks. Never run attract inside an active Hub session or a paused live match.\n\nUse the real game systems for the showcase, with permitted AI cheats and fallback resets so it never gets stuck. The acceptance playthrough uses legitimate driving separately; a cheating attract replay is not evidence that the game is completable.\n    // the sharpest bend ahead (its turn a unit along the line) and the speed it allows; faster than that, it brakes\n    let k = 0; for (let a = 0; a < 4; a++) { const q0 = P.at(R._d + 20 + a * 35), q1 = P.at(R._d + 55 + a * 35); k = Math.max(k, Math.abs(angDiff(q0.angle, q1.angle)) / 35); }\n    const vmax = Math.sqrt(ai.grip / Math.max(k, 1e-4)), leader = this.racers.reduce((a, b) => b.prog > a.prog ? b : a), gap = (leader.prog - R.prog) / L;\n    const it = { steer: clamp(diff * 2.4, -1, 1), gas: car.speed < vmax ? 1 : 0, brake: car.speed > vmax + 15 ? clamp((car.speed - vmax) / 50, .3, 1) : 0, top: ai.skill * (1 + clamp(gap * 8, -.5, 1) * ai.rubber) };\n    if (car.o.kart && vmax < car.o.top * .85 && car.speed > car.o.top * .55 && Math.abs(diff) > .15) { it.drift = true; it.steer = clamp(it.steer + Math.sign(diff) * .3, -1, 1); }\n    // stuck against a wall: back away, turned\n    if (car.speed < 12 && this.go) R._stuck += dt; else R._stuck = 0;\n    if (R._stuck > 1.2) { R._back = .8; R._stuck = 0; }\n    if (R._back > 0) { R._back -= dt; return { steer: -Math.sign(diff || 1), brake: 1 }; }\n    if (R.item && (R.item === 'boost' ? k < .004 : R.item === 'shot' ? this.racers.some(Q => Q !== R && Q.prog - R.prog > 20 && Q.prog - R.prog < 160) : this.racers.some(Q => Q !== R && R.prog - Q.prog > 10 && R.prog - Q.prog < 60))) this.use(R);\n    return it;\n  }\n  // where a racer is across the track: -1 left edge .. 1 right\n  _lane(R) { const p = this.path.at(R._d); return ((R.car.x - p.x) * -Math.sin(p.angle) + (R.car.y - p.y) * Math.cos(p.angle)) / (this.o.width * .55); }\n  /** fire a racer's item: boost (now), shot (ahead along its heading), hazard (behind it) */\n  use(R) {\n    const c = R.car, it = R.item; if (!it) return; R.item = null;\n    if (it === 'boost') c.boost(1, .45);\n    else if (it === 'shot') this.shots.push({ x: c.x + Math.cos(c.angle) * 12, y: c.y + Math.sin(c.angle) * 12, vx: Math.cos(c.angle) * (c.o.top * 1.6), vy: Math.sin(c.angle) * (c.o.top * 1.6), t: 0, by: R });\n    else this.hazards.push({ x: c.x - Math.cos(c.angle) * 14, y: c.y - Math.sin(c.angle) * 14, t: 0 });\n  }\n  /** one step: the countdown, every car, bumps, items, progress, laps, wrong way, places, the finish */\n  update(dt, map) {\n    const L = this.path.length, n = this.o.checkpoints, cars = this.racers.map(R => R.car);\n    this.t += dt; this.go = this.t >= 0;\n    for (const R of this.racers) {\n      const ctl = !this.go ? { gas: 0, brake: 1 } : R.finished ? { gas: .4, steer: R.ai ? this.drive(R, dt).steer : 0 } : R.ai ? this.drive(R, dt) : R.input;\n      R.car.update(dt, map, ctl);\n    }\n    Car.collide(cars.filter(c => !c.isVehicle));   // (the 3D engine's vehicles bump in Rapier)\n    for (const b of this.boxes) { b.t -= dt; if (b.t > 0) continue; for (const R of this.racers) if (!R.item && Math.hypot(R.car.x - b.x, R.car.y - b.y) < R.car.r + 8) { const I = this.o.items; R.item = I[Math.floor(Math.random() * I.length)]; b.t = 2; break; } }\n    for (const s of this.shots) {\n      s.t += dt; s.x += s.vx * dt; s.y += s.vy * dt;\n      if (map && map.collide) { const b = { x: s.x, y: s.y, r: 3 }; if (map.collide(b)) { s.t = 9; continue; } }\n      for (const R of this.racers) if (R !== s.by && Math.hypot(R.car.x - s.x, R.car.y - s.y) < R.car.r + 4) { R.car.spinOut(1); s.t = 9; }\n    }\n    this.shots = this.shots.filter(s => s.t < 3);\n    for (const h of this.hazards) for (const R of this.racers) if (h.t < 9 && Math.hypot(R.car.x - h.x, R.car.y - h.y) < R.car.r + 4 && R.car.onGround) { R.car.spinOut(.9); h.t = 9; }\n    this.hazards = this.hazards.filter(h => h.t < 9);\n    for (const R of this.racers) {\n      // progress round the line (unwrapped), checkpoints in order, laps\n      const d = this.path.nearest(R.car.x, R.car.y); let dd = d - R._d; if (dd > L / 2) dd -= L; if (dd < -L / 2) dd += L; R.prog += dd; R._d = d;\n      if (R.finished) continue;\n      if (this.go) { R.time += dt; R.lapT += dt; }\n/*!\n * my-3D2dge v0.89.1  (My \"3D\" 2D Game Engine)\n * Procedural pixel-puppet engine for every 2D perspective.\n * https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge  (MIT License)\n *\n * READ THIS FIRST (humans and AI models extending a my-3D2dge game)\n * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * 0. EVERY GAME STARTS WITH  const E = My3D2dge;  and then uses E.Game, E.px, ...\n * 1. WORLD SPACE IS 3D. x = east, y = south, z = up. One floor tile = 16 units.\n *    Game logic never thinks about screen pixels.\n * 2. A VIEW turns world points into screen pixels. Swap the view and the whole game\n *    re-renders from another angle: E.VIEWS.iso, .threequarter, .topdown,\n *    .brawler, .side  (or build your own: new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost)).\n * 3. CHARACTERS ARE RIGS, NOT SPRITES. E.Humanoid and E.Blob recompute their\n *    joints from math every step, in 3D, then project them. That is why they move\n *    fluidly and can face any angle in any view without extra art. Small things\n *    (coins, bullets, icons) can be pixel sprites written as strings: E.sprite().\n * 4. PIXEL RULES. Draw with E.px (rect, line, disc, ell, poly, sprite...). Every shape\n *    snaps to whole pixels. Never use ctx.arc / ctx.stroke / ctx.fillText on the game\n *    buffer: they anti-alias and break the look. Translucency and glow go through\n *    px.blend / polyDither / ddisc / r.glowDisc: stepped alpha and blend modes like PS1\n *    hardware (E.style.trans = 'dither' switches to NES / Genesis style dithering).\n * 5. FRAME ORDER (handled by the Renderer):\n *      background (r.sky, map.drawFloor or level.draw) -> decals (ground marks, shadows)\n *      -> sorted queue (walls, props, actors, particles) -> lighting -> overlays -> present\n *    Anything that can be in front of or behind something else goes through\n *    r.queue(x, y, z, fn), r.actor(x, y, z, drawFn, opts) or r.sprite(...).\n * 6. TIME. game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) }) or game.start({ scenes, scene }).\n *    update runs in exact fixed steps: each 1/120 s of real time is one update with\n *    dt = 1/120 * timeScale, so motion is identical at 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz (game.alpha =\n *    the part of a step left over). game.freeze(s) = hit-stop, game.shake(n) = screen shake,\n *    game.timeScale = slow motion, game.paused = pause, game.after(s, fn) = timer.\n * 7. LEVELS. Top-down / isometric / brawler floors: E.TileMap (walls on the ground\n *    plane). Side-scrollers: E.PlatformMap + E.Platformer (tiles on the x/z plane,\n *    solid, one-way, ladders, hazards, moving platforms; crouching, ledges, clinging, water, ropes, bars, swinging and\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
